   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear shiny users,<div><br></div><div>I would like to ask a it of help for my first shiny application. Since I do not have computer science background, my question is</div><div>probably trivial to many users out there. But if anybody can provide some clues, that would be much appreciated.</div><div><br></div><div>I am trying to draw interactive graphics for average temperature in London, Paris, and Berlin. I downloaded the data from</div><div><a href=\"http://www.wunderground.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.wunderground.com&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792404824940000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0uEJBdqgYBO-ciRXuHSunt\">www.wunderground.com</a> using weatherData package. I am using examples from Chris Beeley&#39;s book and Rstudio in order to</div><div>design my own application.</div><div><br></div><div>In my server.R, I upload three data files first. Then, I have sidebar with controls to select a dataset. I also have date range in the sidebar.</div><div>Once users choose a location and time range, I am asking R to do some data arrangement and pass the object, passData for the next</div><div>operation. By the time, R reaches renderplot( ), I am assuming that R has a right data frame and produce a graphic using ggplot2. But,</div><div>I receive the following error message.</div><div><br></div><div>Error in print(theGraph) : object &#39;theGraph&#39; not found<br></div><div><br></div><div>This makes me think that R may not have a right data frame to generate an output graphic. I wonder if anybody can spot what I am doing</div><div>wrong here. Thank you very much for your attention and support.</div><div><br></div><div>Sincerely,</div><div>Kota</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "EdfY2mSKPiwJ",
   "from": "Ramnath Vaidyanathan",
   "author": "c6ca7877bef91eef",
   "date": "2014-02-13T15:57:19Z",
   "subject": "Re: No output graphics",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Here is the source of your problem (most probably). <div><br></div><div>In ui.R you write<div><br></div><div><div>selectInput(&quot;dataset&quot;, &quot;Choose a location&quot;,</div><div>  choices = c(&quot;Berlin Tigel Airport&quot;, &quot;London City Airport&quot;, &quot;Paris Charles De Gaulle&quot;)</div><div>)</div><div><br></div><div>and in server.R you check</div><div><br></div><div>input$dataset == &quot;Berlin&quot;</div><div><br></div><div>There is no way for the server to know that when you choose &quot;Berlin Tigel Airport&quot;, it needs to use &quot;Berlin&quot; on the server side. </div><div><br></div><div>You can fix this by modifying your selectInput. Read ? selectInput to know what happens when you use a named vector in selectInput.</div><div><br></div><div><div>selectInput(&quot;dataset&quot;, &quot;Choose a location&quot;,</div><div>  choices = c(</div><div>    &quot;Berlin Tigel Airport&quot; = &quot;berlin&quot;,</div><div>    &quot;London City Airport&quot; = &quot;london&quot;,</div><div>    &quot;Paris Charles De Gaulle&quot; = &quot;paris&quot;</div><div>  )</div><div>)</div></div><div><br></div><div>Hope this helps.</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><br>On Thursday, February 13, 2014 12:33:26 AM UTC-5, kota wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Dear shiny users,<div><br></div><div>I would like to ask a it of help for my first shiny application. Since I do not have computer science background, my question is</div><div>probably trivial to many users out there. But if anybody can provide some clues, that would be much appreciated.</div><div><br></div><div>I am trying to draw interactive graphics for average temperature in London, Paris, and Berlin. I downloaded the data from</div><div><a href=\"http://www.wunderground.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.wunderground.com&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792404824941000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0O9ImhcJ5D0fvCfrET-ZbN\">www.wunderground.com</a> using weatherData package. I am using examples from Chris Beeley&#39;s book and Rstudio in order to</div><div>design my own application.</div><div><br></div><div>In my server.R, I upload three data files first. Then, I have sidebar with controls to select a dataset. I also have date range in the sidebar.</div><div>Once users choose a location and time range, I am asking R to do some data arrangement and pass the object, passData for the next</div><div>operation. By the time, R reaches renderplot( ), I am assuming that R has a right data frame and produce a graphic using ggplot2. But,</div><div>I receive the following error message.</div><div><br></div><div>Error in print(theGraph) : object &#39;theGraph&#39; not found<br></div><div><br></div><div>This makes me think that R may not have a right data frame to generate an output graphic. I wonder if anybody can spot what I am doing</div><div>wrong here. Thank you very much for your attention and support.</div><div><br></div><div>Sincerely,</div><div>Kota</div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
